+pysemver sort
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Sort versions, oldest first.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver sort [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    Versions to sort. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard in*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The sorted versions are printed on *standard out* as plain text, one
+version per line, oldest first. This makes the output easy to process in
+shell scripts, for example with ``while read``::
+
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+    $ printf '1.10.0\n\n1.2.0\n' | pysemver sort
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+
+If any version is not a valid semver version, nothing is printed on
+standard out; you get an error message and a return code != 0.
+
+
+pysemver latest
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Print the highest version which is not a prerelease.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver latest [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    Versions to choose from. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard in*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The result is printed on *standard out* as JSON (a string), so it can be
+consumed with ``fromJSON()`` in GitHub Actions or with ``jq``::
+
+    $ pysemver latest 1.2.0 2.0.0-rc.1 1.10.0
+    "1.10.0"
+    $ printf '1.10.0\n1.2.0\n' | pysemver latest
+    "1.10.0"
+
+If a version is invalid, or no version without a prerelease part is
+given, nothing is printed on standard out; you get an error message and
+a return code != 0.
+
+
 Return Code
 -----------
 
